The boundary decides **what code runs where** and how much JavaScript the browser downloads.

## Roles

| | Server Component | Client Component |
|---|---|---|
| Directive | Default in the App Router | `"use client"` at top of file |
| Runs | Server (build or request) | Server for first render, then browser |
| Can use | Database, secrets, async/await | State, effects, events, browser APIs |
| In the bundle | No | Yes |

## What can cross the boundary

Props passed from server to client are **serialised**. Safe values are primitives, plain objects and arrays, dates and similar supported types, and React elements (such as `children`). **Not allowed**: ordinary functions, class instances with methods, database connections, and anything containing secrets. Server functions marked for the framework (server actions) are a special, supported case.

```tsx
// ProductPage.tsx (server component) - illustrative
import { AddToCart } from "./AddToCart"; // client component

export default async function ProductPage({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const product = await getProduct(id); // server-only data access
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <AddToCart sku={product.sku} /> {/* serialisable prop */}
    </main>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// AddToCart.tsx
"use client";
import { useState } from "react";
export function AddToCart({ sku }: { sku: string }) {
  const [busy, setBusy] = useState(false);
  return <button disabled={busy} onClick={() => setBusy(true)}>Add</button>;
}
```

{{diagram:boundary}}

## Guidelines

- Keep `use client` on the smallest interactive leaf, not on the page.
- A client component cannot import a server component, but can receive one as `children`.
- Use a server-only guard for modules that must never reach the browser; verify the current docs for the recommended mechanism.

## Common mistakes

- Marking the root layout as client, making the whole tree client-side.
- Passing a whole database record containing private fields to a client component; it becomes visible in the page payload.
- Putting secrets in a module imported by a client component.
- Reaching for global client state when a server fetch would do.

Server Actions let a component call a server function, commonly from a form. The convenience hides the fact that the framework exposes an **endpoint** that anyone can call with crafted requests. Details evolve, so verify current Next.js docs for built-in protections.

## Threats and controls

| Threat | Control |
|---|---|
| Unauthenticated calls | Check the session inside **every** action, not only in the page |
| Broken object-level authorisation | Verify the user may act on this record id |
| Tampered input | Validate and coerce with a schema on the server |
| CSRF | Framework origin checks and same-site cookies; confirm current behaviour and proxy host headers |
| Mass assignment | Pick allowed fields explicitly |
| Abuse and replay | Rate limits, idempotency keys for payments or orders |
| Leaking data in errors | Return safe messages, log details server-side |

```ts
// illustrative
"use server";
import { z } from "zod";

const Input = z.object({ orderId: z.string().uuid(), note: z.string().max(500) });

export async function addOrderNote(raw: unknown) {
  const session = await requireSession();           // authenticate
  const { orderId, note } = Input.parse(raw);       // validate
  const order = await getOrder(orderId);
  if (order.customerId !== session.userId) {        // authorise the resource
    throw new Error("Forbidden");
  }
  await saveNote(orderId, note);
}
```

{{diagram:action}}

## Design notes

- Hidden form fields and bound arguments are **user-controlled**; re-derive identity and prices on the server.
- Behind a proxy or CDN, make sure host and origin headers are forwarded correctly or legitimate calls may fail the origin check.
- Prefer a shared service layer so actions and route handlers use the same authorisation code.

## Common mistakes

- Assuming a page-level login check protects the action.
- Trusting a `userId` argument.
- Skipping validation because TypeScript types exist; types vanish at runtime.
- Returning raw exceptions or database errors to the client.

Route handlers (files such as `route.ts` in the App Router) define HTTP endpoints using web-standard Request and Response objects. Verify current docs for caching defaults of GET handlers, which changed between versions.

## When to use

| Use case | Route handler? |
|---|---|
| Payment or CMS webhook | Yes |
| Mobile app or third party consuming JSON | Yes, or a dedicated API service |
| File export or signed download | Yes |
| Data for your own server component | No, call the service function directly |
| Mutations from your own forms | Server action or handler, per team standard |

```ts
// app/api/webhooks/payments/route.ts - illustrative
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const body = await req.text();
  const signature = req.headers.get("x-signature");
  if (!verifySignature(body, signature)) {
    return new Response("Invalid", { status: 401 });
  }
  await handlePaymentEvent(JSON.parse(body)); // idempotent
  return new Response("ok");
}
```

## Boundary design

{{diagram:layers}}

- **Presentation** (components) never touches the database directly except through the service layer.
- **Service layer** holds rules, authorisation and orchestration; framework-agnostic and testable.
- **Handlers and actions** are adapters: parse, call service, format.
- If many consumers or teams need the API, a **separate service** is cleaner than growing handlers inside the UI app.

## Common mistakes

- A server component fetching its own route handler over HTTP, adding latency and a failure point.
- Business rules written inside handlers, duplicated in actions.
- No signature verification and no idempotency on webhooks.
- Unplanned caching of GET handlers returning user-specific data; verify current behaviour.

Middleware intercepts matched requests before the route handles them. Naming, file conventions and runtime options have changed across versions, so **verify current Next.js docs**.

## Good uses

| Use | Why it fits |
|---|---|
| Redirects and rewrites | Cheap and early |
| Locale or region routing | Reads headers or cookies |
| A/B bucketing | Sets a cookie, rewrites path |
| Adding security or tracing headers | Consistent across routes |
| Coarse "is there a session cookie" gating | Fast UX redirect |

```ts
// illustrative
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import type { NextRequest } from "next/server";

export function middleware(req: NextRequest) {
  const hasSession = req.cookies.has("session");
  if (!hasSession && req.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith("/account")) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/login", req.url));
  }
  return NextResponse.next();
}
export const config = { matcher: ["/account/:path*"] };
```

## Limits

- Constrained runtime: not all Node APIs or libraries work; keep bundles small.
- Runs on many requests, so latency adds up; scope it with a matcher.
- Not a place for slow database or heavy session verification.
- It sees the request, not the final data, so it cannot authorise a specific record.
- Historic vulnerabilities in middleware-only authorisation show why defence in depth matters.

{{diagram:mw}}

## Common mistakes

- Treating middleware as the sole authentication and authorisation layer.
- Matching every path including static assets.
- Calling slow upstream services on each request.
- Putting business logic that needs Node-only libraries there.

The App Router uses **file conventions** to place error boundaries in the route tree. Verify exact props and behaviour in the docs for your version.

## Files

| File | Scope | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `error.tsx` | Its segment and children | Client component; gets the error and a reset function |
| `not-found.tsx` | Segment or app | Rendered when the not-found function is called or no route matches; 404 status |
| `global-error.tsx` | Root layout failures | Replaces the root layout when active, so it defines its own html and body |
| `loading.tsx` | Segment | Suspense fallback, not an error file but part of the same tree |

```tsx
// app/products/[id]/error.tsx - illustrative
"use client";
export default function Error({ error, reset }: { error: Error; reset: () => void }) {
  return (
    <div role="alert">
      <p>We could not load this product.</p>
      <button onClick={reset}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// in a server component
import { notFound } from "next/navigation";
const product = await getProduct(id);
if (!product) notFound();
```

{{diagram:err}}

## Practices

- Nest error boundaries close to risky widgets so the rest of the page survives.
- In production, error messages from server components are generally redacted for the client; rely on logs and a digest or id for correlation. Verify current behaviour.
- Expected errors (validation, not found) should be handled as values or with `notFound`, not thrown as exceptions.
- Report errors to your monitoring tool from the boundary and from the server.

## Common mistakes

- One top-level boundary only, so any failure blanks the page.
- Showing raw error messages containing internals.
- Forgetting that errors thrown after streaming starts cannot change the status code.
- Not testing the error UI.
